---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="Privacy"
  description="How Dustbound handles your data — your checklist stays on your phone, Pairing is optional, and the Support form is handled by Formspree."
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6 prose-invert" data-testid="privacy-page">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-black text-ink">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted">Last updated: 22 August 2026</p>

    <section class="mt-8 space-y-3 text-muted" data-testid="privacy-intro">
      <p>
        Dustbound is made by <strong class="text-ink">Ingenium Software Engineering</strong>.
        This page is about what the app and <a href="https://dustbound.app/">dustbound.app</a> do with your information.
      </p>
      <p>
        Privacy questions: <a href="mailto:privacy@ingeniumsoftware.dev">privacy@ingeniumsoftware.dev</a>.
        See also our <a href="/cookies/">cookie policy</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 text-muted" data-testid="privacy-app">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">In short</h2>
      <p>
        Your checklist lives on your phone. We don’t ask you to create an account, and we don’t upload
        what you’ve Collected or Mastered. Pairing is optional — if you use it, we keep just enough
        to help you find other players and, after you both say yes, share Epic usernames with each other.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 text-muted">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">What stays on your phone</h2>
      <p>
        Dustbound is built to work without us holding a copy of your progress.
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Your checklist</strong> — which Collectibles you’ve marked Collected or Mastered.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">Your settings</strong> — things like Event reminder choices and timezone.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-ink">A local copy of the catalog</strong> — so the list still works when you’re offline.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you back up your phone, that backup may include Dustbound data. That’s your device, not us.
      </p>
      <p>
        The app also downloads the public catalog and Events list so checklists stay current.
        Those downloads don’t include your progress.
      </p>
      <p>
        Event reminders, if you turn them on, are scheduled on your device. We don’t send those from our servers.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 text-muted" data-testid="privacy-pairing">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">Pairing</h2>
      <p>
        Pairing is how you find other Dustbound players to help each other obtain Collectibles —
        trading or indexing, in the language Fortnite players already use.
      </p>
      <p>
        You don’t have to use it. If you never set up a Pairing profile, none of this section applies.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">How it works</h3>
      <p>
        You create a Pairing profile on your phone: a display name, your region and timezone
        (so we can match you with people in a similar place and time), and your Epic username.
      </p>
      <p>
        From there you can post what you need help with, or look through other people’s requests
        and offer to help. When two of you line up on the same Collectible, you both confirm.
        Only then do you see each other’s Epic username, so you can find one another in-game.
        Afterward you mark how it went — that’s how Reputation is built.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">What we use to make that work</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Display name, region, timezone, and whether you’re open to Pairing</strong> —
          so other players can find you and so matching makes sense.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Help requests</strong> — which Collectible you need, whether it’s a trade or an index,
          and enough status to keep the request moving.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-ink">Reputation</strong> — a score from how Pairings closed, shown on Pairing screens.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The app also has a private install key so this copy of Dustbound can act as you, without an account.
        We don’t keep a usable copy of that key.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">Your Epic username</h3>
      <p>
        Your Epic username stays readable on your phone. When you save your Pairing profile,
        we also store an <strong class="text-ink">encrypted</strong> copy so a later match can unlock.
        We can’t read that copy on our own.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other player sees your Epic username only after <strong class="text-ink">you both confirm</strong>.
        Until then, they see your display name — not the name they’d search for in Fortnite.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">What other players see</h3>
      <p>
        Before you both confirm: your display name, and enough about the request to decide whether to help.
        After you both confirm: your Epic username.
      </p>
      <p>
        Leaderboards use your display name and Pairing scores. They’re off unless you turn them on.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">Pairing alerts</h3>
      <p>
        You can get a ping when something happens on a request you’re part of — someone offering help,
        a confirm waiting, or a Pairing that just closed. That’s optional, and you can turn it off in Settings.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-accent">Keeping and deleting Pairing data</h3>
      <p>
        We keep Pairing data only as long as it’s needed to run matching and history.
        To ask us to delete it, email <a href="mailto:privacy@ingeniumsoftware.dev">privacy@ingeniumsoftware.dev</a>.
        Clearing the app or uninstalling Dustbound removes what’s on your phone; it doesn’t automatically
        erase what Pairing already stored for matching.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your checklist still stays on your phone, even if you use Pairing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 text-muted">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">What we don’t collect</h2>
      <p>
        No Dustbound account. No ads. No analytics from us. We don’t ask for your photos, contacts,
        precise location, or microphone. Buying the app is handled by Google Play or the App Store —
        their checkout, their rules.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-4 text-muted" data-testid="privacy-website">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">This website</h2>
      <p>
        There is no signup list or newsletter. The store buttons are ordinary links to Google Play and the App Store.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you write through the <a href="/support/">Support</a> page, <strong class="text-ink">Formspree</strong>
        delivers that message — your email and what you wrote — so we can reply. See
        <a href="https://formspree.io/legal/privacy-policy/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Formspree’s Privacy Policy</a>.
        To ask us to delete a support message, email
        <a href="mailto:privacy@ingeniumsoftware.dev">privacy@ingeniumsoftware.dev</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        We don’t set our own cookies and we don’t use analytics. See the <a href="/cookies/">cookie policy</a>.
        The Events list loads the public catalog and sprite artwork from GitHub Pages.
        Whoever hosts the site may see the usual connection details any website sees when a page loads.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-3 text-muted">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">Children</h2>
      <p>
        Dustbound isn’t aimed at children, and we don’t knowingly collect personal information from them.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 space-y-3 text-muted">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">If we change this</h2>
      <p>
        We’ll update the date at the top. The current version always lives at <a href="https://dustbound.app/privacy/">dustbound.app/privacy</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</Layout>
